WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.309 --> 00:00:06.960
Speaker 1: David, should free speech apply to&nbsp;
social media? The First Amendment only protects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.960 --> 00:00:13.840
against government censorship, but should it&nbsp;
protect free speech in the way that social media&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:21.280
is speech as well? Listen, there's basically no&nbsp;
I mean, the First Amendment means what the First&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.280 --> 00:00:31.120
Amendment means. It comes to encompass government&nbsp;
limiting speech. If you want a First Amendment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:38.400
to also cover what you write on Twitter so Twitter&nbsp;
can't ban you, you either have to change what is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.400 --> 00:00:42.560
in the First Amendment to have it include&nbsp;
private companies, which would be extremely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.360
anti libertarian, quite frankly. I mean,&nbsp;
if that's a big regulation of business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:51.600
or you have to make social media part of&nbsp;
government, you have to nationalize social media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.600 --> 00:00:56.320
I don't see that happening. That seems&nbsp;
like a complete and total nonstarter. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.320 --> 00:01:02.800
there's a couple of other things that I think&nbsp;
are important to think about if you do. Include&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.840 --> 00:01:11.840
social media companies as governed by the First&nbsp;
Amendment and by the government more broadly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:16.880
you are restricting the First Amendment&nbsp;
and free speech rights of those companies.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.880 --> 00:01:21.120
What do I mean by that? Well, think&nbsp;
about it. If you are Twitter and you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:27.120
can have terms of service, you can enforce the&nbsp;
terms of service as you see fit as long as you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.840
not violating any law. Twitter wouldn't be able&nbsp;
to say, well, we don't allow anyone we believe to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:37.840
be black to tweet. That would violate specific&nbsp;
nondiscrimination law. But if you just blanket&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.840 --> 00:01:41.040
say now, you've got to let people say whatever&nbsp;
they want, you can enforce your terms of service.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.760 --> 00:01:47.040
That is a limitation on Twitter's First Amendment&nbsp;
rights. That is a limitation on Twitter's ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:51.520
to govern itself as a private business, which&nbsp;
Republicans claim to want. So not only does&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:56.880
it not make sense to apply First Amendment&nbsp;
protections when it comes to social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:01.120
not only would you have to change the First&nbsp;
Amendment or nationalize social media, you're also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:07.840
restricting the speech of those companies. Part&nbsp;
of free speech is a company and you can define.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.680 --> 00:02:15.040
How your platform works, what what happens on&nbsp;
your property, in your business, on your platform,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:23.280
as long as it doesn't violate any law, forcing&nbsp;
people to tolerate speech that Twitter doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:23.280 --> 00:02:29.920
want on their platform is a restriction on&nbsp;
Twitter. You are coming down on Twitter. You&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:35.040
are you're regulating Twitter in a way that&nbsp;
is not actually justified by the law. So&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:41.440
a couple of different things. First of all. If&nbsp;
the government wanted to regulate online speech.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:50.560
Would they have a responsibility to provide some&nbsp;
kind of alternative platform of sorts? If so,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:03:01.360
isn't that very problematic? Second, might we&nbsp;
not benefit from some kind of publicly governed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:09.760
infrastructure for helping people to assess the&nbsp;
accuracy of the things that they read online? And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:15.520
this really goes back to education. A lot of this&nbsp;
stuff that we're dealing with about shouldn't and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.520 --> 00:03:20.640
anti vax is just ideas. It's speech. It should&nbsp;
be allowed. Well, no, it's actually dangerous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.640 --> 00:03:24.720
to public health. It shouldn't be allowed.&nbsp;
Putting aside the legality of whether Twitter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:28.560
can say no anti back stuff, whether YouTube&nbsp;
can say no anti back stuff, putting aside the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.560 --> 00:03:34.960
legality because it is legal, wouldn't we all be&nbsp;
better off? With a much more educated population&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.680 --> 00:03:42.320
such that they never start even falling for the&nbsp;
anti back stuff and way fewer people even think&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:48.000
to spread the anti VAX stuff, and that is an area&nbsp;
where I think the government could play a role&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.040 --> 00:03:57.360
furthering media literacy. I'm open to the idea&nbsp;
of a nonpartizan commission on media literacy. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.160
question, of course, is, is it always going to be&nbsp;
just influenced by whoever's in the White House at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:05.600
the time? It should be protected from that. And&nbsp;
I don't know if that means it's governed by an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.600 --> 00:04:09.920
equal number of Republicans and Democrats, then&nbsp;
you just kind of have a partizan fight over it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:14.240
or could there be some other way? I think&nbsp;
it could be done. And I think that that that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:20.080
would be a great thing to do. And then lastly,&nbsp;
by the way. A lot of the real cases to be made&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:27.200
against some of these companies are really&nbsp;
monopoly cases. We have antitrust law on the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:33.520
books that there has been very little appetite in&nbsp;
enforcing for a very, very long time. Republicans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:40.480
now want it enforced, but only as it impacts&nbsp;
social media. They don't care about it in oil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.480 --> 00:04:44.320
They don't care about it in energy and in other&nbsp;
areas. They want it enforced with social media.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:45.120 --> 00:04:50.960
Without commenting on that hypocrisy, if you&nbsp;
want to more stringently regulate social media,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:57.280
we have an apparatus to do it. And it's called&nbsp;
antitrust law. We've done interviews with experts&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.280 --> 00:05:01.360
on it in the past. There seems to be&nbsp;
very little appetite to actually do that.

